[Context: Cours Programme 2026/2027 > Semestre 1 > **UE AI Game Programming (EN)** - 3 ECTS]
# Cours Programme 2026/2027  
## Semestre 1  
### **UE AI Game Programming (EN)** - 3 ECTS  
- Lecteur: M. Regin
- This course presents different techniques to program games for one or two players. For two-player games, only full information games will be considered.
- The students will have to develop, in groups of two, a program that will simulate a human player.
A competition between the programs will be organized and it will determine the notes.

---

[Context: Cours Programme 2026/2027 > Semestre 2 > **UE Logic for AI** (EN) - 3 ECTS]
### **UE Logic for AI** (EN) - 3 ECTS  
- Lecteur: M. Tettamanzi
- The objective of this course is to provide a general background in Logic to enable access to more advanced topics in computer science and, more specifically, in artificial intelligence. Intelligent systems in the real world have to deal with issues like uncertainty, imprecision, and incompleteness. Some prominent logical tools that can be used to approach these issues are thus discussed.
- **Lectures**
- Part I. Basics
- Propositional Logic: syntax and semantics
- First Order Predicate Logic: syntax and semantics
- Natural Deduction
- Unification and Resolution
- Part II. Non-Monotonic Logic and Approximate Reasoning
- Argumentation Theory
- Belief Revision and Update
- Fuzzy Logic
- Possibility Theory

---

[Context: Cours Programme 2026/2027 > Semestre 1 > **UE Model Checking for AI** (EN) - 3 ECTS]
### **UE Model Checking for AI** (EN) - 3 ECTS  
- Lecteur: Mme. De Maria
- This course covers methods and tools for automatically checking
whether a system (software or hardware) meets a given specification.
- Course program
- The need for formal methods to verify hardware and software systems
- Reactive systems
- The model-checking process and its advantages
- System modeling (Kripke structure)
- Specification modeling (CTL and LTL temporal logics, fairness)
- Model-checking algorithms and their complexity
- Improvements: symbolic model-checking and on-the-fly model-checking
- Realistic examples

---

[Context: Cours Programme 2026/2027 > Semestre 1 > **Problem Solving** (EN) - 3 ECTS]
### **Problem Solving** (EN) - 3 ECTS  
- Lecteur: Mme. Pelleau
- This course aims to demonstrate how real-life problems can be solved, such as gate allocation for airplanes, constrained pathfinding, etc. Various types of methods will be presented, such as greedy algorithms, local search, and constraint programming.
- This course is divided into 4 main parts:
1. Modelization
2. Greedy Algorithms
3. Local Search
4. Constraint Programming

---

[Context: Cours Programme 2026/2027 > Semestre 2 > **Programming Paradigms**(EN) - 3 ECTS]
### **Programming Paradigms**(EN) - 3 ECTS  
- Lecteur: Mme. De Maria
- A programming paradigm is a set of concepts and methods that determine what is permitted or forbidden in programming. While it does not change what can be expressed, it fundamentally shapes how we express it.
This course introduces the main classical paradigms (imperative, functional, and object-oriented) and examines their underlying principles and practical implications. Students will learn to understand and implement the core ideas of each paradigm within minimalist frameworks to grasp their operational mechanisms. By comparing these paradigms, students will develop the ability to analyze their impact on software design, reasoning, and the overall style of programming.

---

[Context: Cours Programme 2026/2027 > Semestre 2 > **Scalable Data Science**(EN) - 3 ECTS]
### **Scalable Data Science**(EN) - 3 ECTS  
- Lecteur: M. Lozes
- Ce cours vise à donner les bases théoriques et pratiques pour le traitement distribué des données à des fins d'analyse statistique

---

[Context: Cours Programme 2026/2027 > Semestre 2 > **Computational Complexity**(EN) - 3 ECTS]
### **Computational Complexity**(EN) - 3 ECTS  
- Lecteur: E. Formenti
- La complexité computationnelle est une notion fondamentale en informatique qui essaye de comparer/classer les algorithmes par rapport à des fonctions de coût des ressources demandées par leur exécution (complète). La complexité d’un algorithme est donc une mesure de la qualité et comme toute mesure elle a besoin d’un système de référence. Les machines de Turing vont être notre principal système de référence.

---

